{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Original lower bound specialized to a single 1-unit witness: P-D54 only makes an informal, qualified general claim that feedforward networks 'require many more neurons' (noting exceptions such as CNNs for image recognition), and P-H10 describes only the size of the unrolling-through-time construction ('copying the network 100 times' for 100 timesteps). Target strengthens this into a universal lower bound over all functionally equivalent acyclic systems, while narrowing the benchmark recurrent system to one fixed 1-unit Boolean OR-latch (d15) rather than an arbitrary k-unit recurrent network (for which unrolling yields T * k units). This matches the author's explicit acknowledgment that Target is their own lower bound for the one-unit example."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Abstract synchronous 1-step-delay model (S1) vs. neural networks: S1 replaces biological or artificial neurons with arbitrary finite collections of units having unrestricted (even infinite) state types A3 n and arbitrary update/readout functions a5, a6, which strengthens the lower bound. At the same time, S1 idealizes temporal processing as discrete synchronous ticks receiving one Boolean input per step with uniform 1-timestep delays per edge, excluding multi-step conduction delays or static spatial presentation of all T inputs at once (for which a single OR-neuron would suffice)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Feedforwardness via global functional dependence (N.D9): acyclicity is defined on the functional dependency graph N.D8 quantified over all global states in N.D1 (including unreachable ones), rather than structural/anatomical wiring or dependencies on reachable states alone. Structurally acyclic networks satisfy N.D9, and systems with globally inactive feedback loops are also covered, but systems with cycles active only on unreachable states are excluded."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Equivalence over all prefix lengths <= T (S1.D5): Target assumes agreement with d15 on every input sequence of length at most T (including the empty sequence), which is slightly stronger as a hypothesis than requiring agreement only on sequences of length T (though the lower bound T <= N.d13 would already hold from length-T agreement alone for T >= 1)."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target accurately and soundly formalizes the author's stated lower bound—that any acyclic streaming system matching the 1-unit Boolean latch d15 over T steps requires at least T units—and appropriately supports the size-blowup point in P-D54 and P-H10. The divergences are minor, reflecting the author's acknowledged move from the sources' informal/construction-based remarks to a specific formal lower bound and standard discrete-time dynamical-system idealizations."
}
